The PR adds `using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;` and replaces `[Fact]` with `[TestMethod]`.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="8-9"}

No project under `src/Components` or `eng/` references MSTest. A grep for `MSTest` and `Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools` finds only this file. So the `using` doesn't resolve and the whole E2ETest project fails to compile.

Even if MSTest were referenced, this class is xUnit-based (`ServerTestBase`, `ITestOutputHelper`, `[Theory]`). xUnit doesn't discover `[TestMethod]`, so the test would silently stop running. Quarantining should only add `[QuarantinedTest]`, which is what upstream commit 44dee43c does.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="287-288"}

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="287-288"}
    [Fact]
    [QuarantinedTest("https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/63708/")]

`Navigate(...)` was moved below `Browser.Exists(By.TagName("h1"))`:

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="28-35"}

`_originalH1Element` is now whatever `h1` the browser had from the previous page, or nothing if the page was blank. `Navigate` then does a full page load, so that element goes stale. `Browser.Equal("Redirections", () => _originalH1Element.Text)` keeps throwing `StaleElementReferenceException` or reading the wrong text until it times out.

Every test here also relies on `_originalH1Element` being the `/redirect` page's `h1`, so it can check that enhanced navigation kept the same DOM element. All of them break.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="29-34"}
        await base.InitializeAsync();
        Navigate($"{ServerPathBase}/redirect");

        _originalH1Element = Browser.Exists(By.TagName("h1"));
        Browser.Equal("Redirections", () => _originalH1Element.Text);

The comment still says "See that 'back' takes you to the place from before the redirection", but the call is now `Forward()`:

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="112-115"}

After clicking the link, the browser is at the newest history entry, so `Forward()` does nothing. The next assertion, `Browser.Equal("Redirections", ...)`, waits until it times out, and the test fails for both `[InlineData]` cases. All 8 sibling tests in this file use `Back()` (lines 51, 80, 141, 169, 199, 228, 257 and 282).

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="113-113"}
        Browser.Navigate().Back();

This test checks that a `NavigationException` thrown from `NavigateTo` inside `OnInitializedAsync` (see `Pages/Redirections/` `/redirect/circular`) never becomes an unobserved task exception. The PR changes the switch from `false` to `true`:

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="291"}

With the switch set to `true`, `NavigateTo` doesn't throw. No exception can go unobserved, so the count is always 0 and the test passes even if the bug comes back.

It's also an `AppContext` switch set in-process with no reset. It could leak into other tests run after this one.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="291-291"}
        AppContext.SetSwitch("Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints.NavigationManager.DisableThrowNavigationException", false);

